{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1514933-does-anyone-else-find-vtm-to-be-the-most-depressing-gameline","id":1514933,"title":"Does anyone else find VtM to be the most depressing gameline?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-14T03:52:53-07:00","id":1514933,"title":"Does anyone else find VtM to be the most depressing gameline?","content":"Wraith and Changeling have that reputation, but changelings get to inject some wonder into the world and wraiths aren't told &quot;fuck you&quot; by the narrative itself every five seconds (despite arguably being just as fucked up). Wraith is about facing your troubles, finding catharsis and moving on. Vampire, as it's often portrayed, is about being damned and trying and <i>failing</i> to fight it.<br /> Over there, Mages and Werewolves fight for a better world. All you can do is poison it.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, what I get from VtM when it's portrayed in that manner is that you deserve nothing but suffering or death no matter what you do and your choices are meaningless (because you're a monster regardless), happiness is forbidden, even trying to be a positive influence on the world is a total waste of time, achieving Golconda is basically impossible (assuming it even <i>exists</i>), something as horrifying as suicide is considered a good thing, you're a bad person for getting dragged into a cycle of abuse as a victim in the first place, justice is a fiction, and the World of Darkness was created by and ruled over by a neglectful, sociopathic tyrant who actively wants you to be cursed because some asshole murdered his brother, and then thousands of years later your sire decided to murder somebody and somehow their crimes are also inherently your fault. The fact that you're <i>innocent</i> of that is irrelevant; screw you, enjoy your divine punishment. Should you find a way to undo it, say by getting a powerful mage to help, then apparently the resulting paradox backlash is god starting Gehenna early as a &quot;fuck you&quot;.<br /> <br /> Oh, yeah, and the world is heading for a horrific apocalypse, so everything you're working for is meaningless and unlike, say, the Garou who are flawed heroes, even if you fight it <i>you</i> are an abomination that doesn't deserve to exist and can only taint everything you touch.<br /> <br /> Caring is a waste of time and the world ceasing to exist is probably going to be an improvement, frankly. Putting effort into being a saint is a waste of energy. All that effort you put into being a monster is meaningless, because you could never be anything else anyway. I mean, I tone it down at my table, but <i>geez</i>. The Setites may as well have it right; <i>fuck Ra, fuck the rules, all glory to Sutekh.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T04:45:38-07:00","id":1514941,"title":"","content":"I think that would be the Wraith... facing your troubles, finding catharsis and moving on, yeah, in theory, but really, how many Wraith actually managed to move on? The harrowing was originally a form of &quot;tough love&quot; in the Underworld, true, - intended to help wraiths come to terms with their own deaths when they weren't willing to come to terms with it themselves but upon dying, a wraith might take some initial comfort from the idea that things can't get worse.. but Harrowing quickly puts an end to that belief...<br /> Either Wraith or Fallen...<br /> However, yes, Vampire in it core, you are a damned and good luck, so long and thanks for all the fish...<br /> The Followers of Set's apporach makes much more sense... thats true<br /> <br /> Kindred of the East count as VtM by the way? Or seperate line?<br /> That one is nice and balanced in this regard... humans who died violently and are sent to Yomi, yes, from which they escape back into their bodies and must sustain on chi to survive, BUT strive to fulfill their charmic porpuse and Transcend the mortal plane.<br /> And actually able to succeed - unlike Wraith, your dharmic progress have visable merits, like rising above the need of flesh consuming to blood consuming, then to draw chi from breath, then from the world itself, like a sponge. Maybe it take a millenium or ten, but at least you can see, worth it and you have a chance (and as a freebi you actually are the member of the Divine order and even the Hengeyokai and the Beast Courts have a cold relationship with your kind, admiting, meh, they have their porpuse in the grand scheme of things<br /> Wraith are traumatised\u200b<br /> <br /> Anyhow, I think you are a little bit... harsh with Vampire<br /> The end of Beckett's Jyhad Diary summaries it nicely:<br /> <br /> <i>An Antediluvian spoke within my mind just the other night. It told me:<br /> \u201cOur greatest Curse is to live forever, and never feel the urgency of mortality. This is why the Jyhad exists. Without hate for each other, jealousy for what we each possess, and petty grudges to keep us awake at night, we would sleep for eternity.\u201d<br /> That same night, a fledgling said the same thing, or words to that effect.<br /> I replied to both with a laugh and a reassurance.<br /> We do not need to vie against one another to find purpose. Enemies are no essential part of a vampire\u2019s makeup, no more than pervasive danger is for a mortal. We convince ourselves that we must compete and sabotage one another to give our existences meaning, but this is the true Jyhad, our being manipulated into thinking so.<br /> We\u2019re none of us so different. Whether dead for 10,000 years or merely one night, we all possess a capacity for growth, whether through learning, exploration, philosophy, alchemy of the blood, religion, or even remaining true to our inner humanity. I\u2019ve seen and experienced much in my last few decades, some of it the definition of evil, yet some enough to inspire and give one hope.<br /> Anatole is one of my firmest friends. He\u2019s existed for nigh on a millennium, and still cares for my wellbeing, even when his condition forces him to act against me.<br /> Lucita, Fatima, Talley, and Theo are all warriors, and all are capable of causing huge harm to me and others. Yet, they\u2019re all capable of humor, acts of largesse and introspection, and they dedicate themselves to causes greater than their personal wellbeing.<br /> Okulos, Aisling, and Hesha are scholars to whom I owe and dedicate huge respect. They give meaning to their lives through study, betterment, and understanding.<br /> I do not use the word \u201clives\u201d in error. We are vampires, and we may be biologically dead, but we also live. Through my extensive journeying I discover the way to remove oneself from the Jyhad is to move \u2014 mentally, physically, socially. Stay alert, stay awake, absorb the life around you, and give some back.<br /> I\u2019ve met Princes and thin-bloods. I\u2019ve stood in presence of Methuselahs and Kindred of dead Clans. I\u2019ve searched tombs and boardrooms.<br /> I\u2019ve listened to the righteous anger of tyrants and the tragic pleading of fledglings.<br /> I have walked the night wearing shades, spilt sweet blood in the height of passion, used my claws like daggers, and felt the love of a cold body turned warm.<br /> Our blood does not flow, but our vitae burns with a desire to experience. We are outsiders, outcasts, misanthropes, monsters, and creatures of the night, yet we persist, and there is little we cannot accomplish.<br /> You who read this need to know that the Jyhad \u2014 our eternal struggle \u2014 is what you make of it.<br /> You can be a beast, you can be an angel with fangs, and you can be so much more besides.<br /> Fear not the strings of puppet masters. Dwell not upon who controls your fate.<br /> We must all face tomorrow. Do so with open eyes and an open heart, and embrace our future.<br /> Never falter. Do not break. There is always another sunset.<br /> Your faithful author,<br /> Beckett</i>\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T04:46:58-07:00","id":1514943,"title":"","content":"Okay, so swallowed by forum, lets try again...<br /> God I HATE this...<br /> <br /> <br /> I think that would be the Wraith... facing your troubles, finding catharsis and moving on, yeah, in theory, but really, how many Wraith actually managed to move on? The harrowing was originally a form of &quot;tough love&quot; in the Underworld, true, - intended to help wraiths come to terms with their own deaths when they weren't willing to come to terms with it themselves but upon dying, a wraith might take some initial comfort from the idea that things can't get worse.. but Harrowing quickly puts an end to that belief...<br /> Either Wraith or Fallen...<br /> However, yes, Vampire in it core, you are a damned and good luck, so long and thanks for all the fish...<br /> The Followers of Set's apporach makes much more sense... tha